// @test: a module resolves its OWN extern "C" declarations after importing a binding (#442)
// @description: A file that declares its own `extern "C"` functions could not use them if it
//   also imported a module that (transitively) imports a binding declaring the same FFI entry
//   point. The registry attributed the name to a module the importer does not scope, so the
//   namespace gate hid it and every call reported `Undefined identifier` -- the importer's own
//   declaration table stopped resolving. The parser models `extern "C" { ... }` as a namespace
//   statement named `__extern_C`, which the registry's per-module declaration bookkeeping could
//   not see through. Now the block's members count as the declaring module's own declarations:
//   an FFI declaration names a process-global C symbol, so the module that writes it resolves
//   it however many other modules declare it. The three-file shape mirrors the report
//   (binding -> module that imports it -> this file); `getpid` is a libc symbol so the
//   collision needs no GPU. Fails with `Undefined identifier: getpid` when the fix is reverted.
// @category: modules, imports, ffi, binding
// @expect-output: mine=ok other=ok
// @expect-return: 0
// @env: VYB_MODULE_PATH=test/modules/ffi_collide_lib

import ffi_middle::{ffi_middle_probe}

extern "C" {
    getpid()<CInt>
}

main()<Int> -> {
    freedom {
        pid<CInt> = getpid()
        if (pid <= 0) {
            return 1
        }
    }
    other<CInt> = ffi_middle_probe()
    if (other <= 0) {
        return 2
    }
    println("mine=ok other=ok")
    return 0
}
